## Boundary decisions

### Code and execution

`src/` contains reusable behavior. Pure analysis kernels have no I/O. Explicit,
named boundary adapters may load the catalog, configuration, or environment,
but must not perform import-time I/O, silently choose output paths, or
orchestrate the pipeline. `workflow/scripts/` adapts declared Snakemake inputs to
that library and writes only declared outputs. Top-level `scripts/` is for
operator commands that a person invokes deliberately.

### Data, QC, and results

- `raw` means acquired and immutable; `processed` means reusable; `results`
  means terminal review or communication output.
- Real raw data use DVC. Small synthetic examples used only by tests stay under
  `tests/fixtures/` in Git.
- `data/interim/` is never a persistence mechanism. Snapshot reusable processed
  data with DVC when another person or a future run must retrieve the exact bytes.
- Generated outputs stay ignored regardless of size. A compact PNG or CSV does
  not become source merely because Git could hold it.
- Pipeline execution logs belong in `results/logs/`; transient scheduler and
  Snakemake internals remain in their ignored tool-managed directories.

### Figures

If code can regenerate a figure, it belongs in `results/figures/`. If loss would
require a person to redraw it, its editable source and source metadata belong in
`assets/figures/<slug>/`. Programmatic composites read authored and generated
panels and write a generated composite to `results/figures/`; a manually
assembled composite is an authored asset.

### Runs and interpretation

The pipeline may always write current, disposable
`results/provenance.json`. Registering a run is a separate, human-approved act:
it appends `metadata/runs.csv` and archives the complete manifest as
`metadata/provenance/<execution_id>.json`. Human interpretation then belongs in
`reporting/writeups/LAB_NOTEBOOK.md`, keyed to that registered execution. These
three records are complementary, not competing run logs.

### Reporting deliveries

Reporting source remains mutable until it is delivered. Freeze any paper,
poster, talk, or writeup at
`reporting/<type>/<artifact>/deliveries/<YYYY-MM-DD>-<milestone>/`. A delivery
contains the exact payload and figures plus `delivery.yaml`, which records their
SHA-256 hashes, source paths, Git and DVC identities, and execution/artifact IDs.
Never modify or reuse a delivery directory. Historical `submissions/` folders
from older template versions remain valid; do not rename them automatically.

```bash
python scripts/freeze_delivery.py \
  --type papers --artifact <slug> --milestone <slug> \
  --payload <built-file> --figure <figure-file>
```

### Bench records

{% if bench_record_authority == "eln" -%}
The electronic lab notebook (ELN) is the authority for executed bench facts.
`lab/` stores reusable protocols and a pointer, not a second transcription of
the record.
{% if bench_record_location %}Configured ELN location: `{{ bench_record_location }}`.
{% else %}**ELN location: UNCONFIGURED.** Do not guess the notebook, project, or
entry. Ask the user before relying on or recording bench facts.
{% endif %}
{% else -%}
This project uses repository bench records. Human-created executed facts belong
in `lab/records/`; reusable protocols remain directly under `lab/`. Agents may
format supplied facts but may never invent dates, measurements, observations,
sample identities, or protocol execution details.
{% endif %}

### Research reasoning

{% if use_lab_tracker -%}
Lab Tracker is the authority for accepted questions, claims, decisions, dead
ends, and pivots. It stores semantic records and pointers; it is not the ELN,
object store, DVC remote, task tracker, run ledger, or canonical home of full
figure files.
{% if lab_tracker_project_id %}Linked Lab Tracker project: `{{ lab_tracker_project_id }}`.
{% else %}**Lab Tracker project: UNCONFIGURED.** Do not guess a project ID. State
the missing link and pause consequential research-facing decisions until a
person identifies the project.
{% endif %}
{% else -%}
Lab Tracker is disabled. Record human-accepted research decisions as dated files
in `notes/decisions/YYYY-MM-DD-short-slug.md`; ordinary `notes/` remain scratch.
Operational truth still belongs in code, configuration, metadata, or reporting,
not in a decision note.
{% endif %}

